{"maxId":197,"entries":{"copy|Delirium":["u1"],"delirium-burden|Burden and recognition":["u2"],"delirium-recognition|Clinical diagnosis and presentation":["u3"],"delirium-motor-subtypes|The three motor presentations of delirium":["u132"],"copy|row:Form|TH|Form":["u133"],"copy|row:Form|TH|Clinical features":["u134"],"copy|row:Form|TH|Recognition and prognosis":["u135"],"c58-motor-hypoactive|row:Hypoactive|TH|Hypoactive":["u136"],"c58-motor-hypoactive|row:Hypoactive|TD|Lethargy and reduced psychomotor functioning; the more common form in older patients.":["u137"],"c58-motor-hypoactive|row:Hypoactive|TD|Often missed or attributed to low mood or fatigue; carries an overall poorer prognosis.":["u138"],"c58-motor-hyperactive|row:Hyperactive|TH|Hyperactive":["u139"],"c58-motor-hyperactive|row:Hyperactive|TD|Agitation, increased vigilance and often hallucinations.":["u140"],"c58-motor-hyperactive|row:Hyperactive|TD|Usually noticed by caregivers or clinicians. Visibility is not proof of a benign course.":["u141"],"c58-motor-mixed|row:Mixed|TH|Mixed":["u142"],"c58-motor-mixed|row:Mixed|TD|Fluctuation between hypoactive and hyperactive forms.":["u143"],"c58-motor-mixed|row:Mixed|TD|Can be difficult to distinguish from psychotic or mood disorders if the fluctuating course is missed; the supplied passage gives no separate outcome estimate.":["u144"],"delirium-on-dementia|Delirium superimposed on dementia":["u145"],"delirium-factors|Predisposition and precipitating factors":["u4"],"ad-currency|Alzheimer therapy: superseded statement and current evidence":["u118"],"c58-predisposing-factors|row:Group|TH|Group":["u5"],"c58-predisposing-factors|row:Group|TH|Factors named in the table":["u6"],"c58-predisposing-factors|row:Predisposing|TH|Predisposing":["u7"],"c58-predisposing-factors|row:Predisposing|TD|Dementia or cognitive impairment; comorbidity/severity of illness; depression; vision and/or hearing impairment; functional impairment; history of transient ischemia or stroke; history of alcohol abuse; history of hypertension; carotid artery disease; history of delirium; age >70.":["u8"],"c58-precipitating-factors|row:Group|TH|Group":["u9"],"c58-precipitating-factors|row:Group|TH|Factors named in the table":["u10"],"c58-precipitating-factors|row:Care and exposures|TH|Care and exposures":["u11"],"c58-precipitating-factors|row:Care and exposures|TD|Drugs: polypharmacy, psychoactive medications, sedatives, hypnotics; physical restraints; indwelling bladder catheter.":["u102"],"c58-precipitating-factors|row:Physiologic|TH|Physiologic":["u13"],"c58-precipitating-factors|row:Physiologic|TD|Elevated BUN/creatinine ratio; elevated serum urea; abnormal serum albumin; abnormal sodium, glucose or potassium; metabolic acidosis.":["u14"],"c58-precipitating-factors|row:Illness and admission|TH|Illness and admission":["u15"],"c58-precipitating-factors|row:Illness and admission|TD|Infection; iatrogenic complications; major surgical procedure (for example, aortic aneurysm repair, noncardiac thoracic surgery or neurosurgery); trauma admission; urgent admission; coma; ICU stay over 10 days.":["u103"],"delirium-medications|Medication associations":["u17"],"delirium-investigation|Looking for the cause":["u18"],"delirium-outcomes|Course and outcomes":["u19"],"delirium-prevention|Prevention and supportive management":["u20"],"delirium-help|Hospital Elder Life Program and team-based prevention":["u146"],"copy|row:Team members|TH|Team members":["u147"],"copy|row:Team members|TH|Roles described in the chapter":["u148"],"c58-help-volunteers|row:Trained volunteers and family members|TH|Trained volunteers and family members":["u149"],"c58-help-volunteers|row:Trained volunteers and family members|TD|Daily orientation, therapeutic recreation activities and feeding assistance.":["u150"],"c58-help-mobility|row:Physical rehabilitation experts and nurses|TH|Physical rehabilitation experts and nurses":["u151"],"c58-help-mobility|row:Physical rehabilitation experts and nurses|TD|Mobilisation and daily exercises to prevent functional decline.":["u152"],"c58-help-nutrition|row:Dietitians|TH|Dietitians":["u153"],"c58-help-nutrition|row:Dietitians|TD|Appropriate caloric intake and oral hydration.":["u154"],"c58-help-specialists|row:Consultant pharmacists, chaplains and social workers|TH|Consultant pharmacists, chaplains and social workers":["u155"],"c58-help-specialists|row:Consultant pharmacists, chaplains and social workers|TD|Specialised medication, spiritual and social expertise.":["u156"],"delirium-drugs|Medication use and cautions":["u21"],"copy|row:Drug|TH|Drug":["u22"],"copy|row:Drug|TH|Source finding":["u23"],"copy|row:Haloperidol|TH|Haloperidol":["u24","u26"],"c58-haloperidol-start|row:Haloperidol|TD|For delirium in older patients, chapter 58 gives a haloperidol starting dose of 0.25 mg orally or parenterally, repeatable every 30 minutes after vital signs are rechecked.":["u104"],"c58-haloperidol-loading|row:Haloperidol|TD|Most antipsychotic-naive older patients should require a total haloperidol loading dose of no more than 2.5 mg. Maintenance is one-half of the loading dose, given in divided doses over the next 24 hours and tapered over the ensuing 48 hours as agitation resolves.":["u105"],"copy|row:Quetiapine|TH|Quetiapine":["u28","u30"],"c58-quetiapine-start|row:Quetiapine|TD|In chapter 58’s low-dose delirium regimen, quetiapine starts at 12.5 mg.":["u106"],"c58-quetiapine-maximum|row:Quetiapine|TD|In that delirium regimen, the quetiapine 24-hour maximum is 25 mg.":["u107"],"copy|row:Olanzapine|TH|Olanzapine":["u32","u34"],"c58-olanzapine-start|row:Olanzapine|TD|In chapter 58’s low-dose delirium regimen, olanzapine starts at 2.5 mg.":["u108"],"c58-olanzapine-maximum|row:Olanzapine|TD|In that delirium regimen, the olanzapine 24-hour maximum is 10 mg.":["u109"],"copy|row:Risperidone|TH|Risperidone":["u36"],"c58-risperidone-maximum|row:Risperidone|TD|In chapter 58’s low-dose delirium regimen, risperidone starts at 0.25–0.5 mg and has a 24-hour maximum of 1.5 mg.":["u110"],"delirium-covid|COVID-19: presentation and barriers to delirium prevention":["u157"],"delirium-reference|The same effect size, separate interventions":["u38"],"copy|row:Intervention|TH|Intervention":["u39"],"copy|row:Intervention|TH|Source finding":["u40"],"copy|row:Targeted prevention|TH|Targeted prevention":["u41"],"c58-reference-targeted|row:Targeted prevention|TD|Implementation of targeted preventive interventions resulted in a 40% risk reduction for delirium in hospitalized older patients compared with standard care.":["u42"],"copy|row:Geriatric consultation after hip fracture|TH|Geriatric consultation after hip fracture":["u43"],"c58-reference-consult|row:Geriatric consultation after hip fracture|TD|Proactive geriatric consultation has been demonstrated to reduce the risk of delirium post hip fracture by 40% in a randomized controlled trial.":["u44"],"copy|CHAPTER 58 / HAZZARD":["u45"],"c58-opening|Look for an acute change from baseline, especially impaired attention.":["u46"],"c58-opening|Identify the precipitating illness and medication burden, support sleep and mobility, and reassess: a quiet patient can be as delirious as an agitated one.":["u47"],"c58-hospital-frequency|Delirium affects as many as half of all people age 65 and older who are hospitalized.":["u48"],"c58-preventable|Delirium is preventable in up to 50% of cases.":["u49"],"c58-unrecognized|Delirium is unrecognized in up to 70% of older patients.":["u50"],"c58-estimate-context|Frequency, diagnostic performance and risk estimates below come from the chapter’s reported studies.":["u51"],"c58-estimate-context|They are not universal probabilities for an individual patient or a substitute for bedside assessment.":["u52"],"c58-icu-setting|The highest prevalence and incidence rates of delirium occur among ventilated intensive care unit patients.":["u53"],"c58-postacute-frequency|Delirium occurs in up to 48% of patients in nursing homes or postacute settings.":["u54"],"c58-end-of-life-frequency|Delirium occurs in up to 83% of all patients at the end of life.":["u55"],"c58-delirium-special-end-of-life|End-of-life care is one of the chapter’s special issues in delirium; the special-issues discussion is not complete without it.":["u187"],"c58-clinical-diagnosis|Delirium is a clinical diagnosis relying on bedside observation, cognitive assessment, and history-taking from a knowledgeable informant to establish a change from baseline functioning.":["u56"],"c58-associated-noncardinal-features|Psychomotor retardation and paranoid delusions are among the frequently associated, noncardinal features of delirium.":["u188"],"c58-associated-noncardinal-features|They supplement the clinical picture; neither replaces the cardinal assessment of attention and acute change.":["u189"],"c58-time-course|Mental status changes in delirium typically occur over hours to days, in contrast to dementia which presents insidiously over weeks to months.":["u57"],"c58-hypoactive|The hypoactive form of delirium is characterized by lethargy and reduced psychomotor functioning and is the more common form in older patients.":["u58"],"c58-hypoactive|Hypoactive delirium often goes unrecognized and carries an overall poorer prognosis than other forms.":["u59"],"c58-motor-subtypes-intro|Delirium has hypoactive, hyperactive and mixed motor presentations.":["u158"],"c58-motor-subtypes-intro|Quietness does not exclude it, and visible agitation does not establish a primary psychiatric disorder.":["u159"],"c58-subsyndromal-delirium|Partial or subsyndromal delirium draws attention to persistent symptoms in older patients, particularly during resolution.":["u160"],"c58-subsyndromal-delirium|These partial forms also adversely affect long-term clinical outcomes.":["u161"],"c58-delirium-dementia-distinction|Distinguishing a longstanding confusional state due to dementia from delirium alone, or delirium superimposed on dementia, is important and often difficult.":["u162"],"c58-delirium-dementia-distinction|Acute change, impaired attention and altered level of consciousness favour delirium over an insidious dementia course.":["u163"],"c58-delirium-dementia-time-axis|Chronic cognitive changes over months to years are most likely due to dementia.":["u164"],"c58-delirium-dementia-time-axis|Abrupt deterioration recognised over hours to weeks may be superimposed on that dementia and should prompt evaluation, including cognitive testing, for delirium.":["u165"],"c58-delirium-dementia-time-axis|Delirium’s onset remains acute; a longer recognition interval does not make it a chronic dementia syndrome.":["u166"],"c58-delirium-psychiatric-differential|If notable delirium features are absent, consider major depression, acute psychotic disorder and other psychiatric disorders.":["u167"],"c58-delirium-psychiatric-differential|The differential also includes nonorganic psychiatric conditions; the chapter’s differential is organised in TABLE 58-4.":["u168"],"c58-ubcam-expansion|UB-CAM means Ultra Brief Confusion Assessment Method, as named in the abbreviation list for FIGURE 58-2.":["u169"],"c58-cam-definition|The Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) algorithm relies on the presence of acute onset and fluctuating course, inattention, and either disorganized thinking or altered level of consciousness.":["u60"],"c58-cam-performance|The Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) algorithm has a sensitivity of 94% to 100%.":["u61"],"c58-cam-performance|The Confusion Assessment Method (CAM) algorithm has a specificity of 90% to 95%.":["u62"],"c58-attention-forward|Inattention can be indicated by an inability to repeat five digits forward.":["u63"],"c58-attention-backward|Inattention can be indicated by an inability to repeat three digits backward.":["u64"],"c58-multimorbidity-predisposition|Multimorbidity is a predisposing factor for delirium, alongside the other vulnerabilities in the chapter’s factor table.":["u190"],"c58-delirium-aetiology-categories|Aetiological categories include infectious, endocrine, neoplastic, metabolic, cardiac and pulmonary disease, with causes both within and outside the CNS.":["u191"],"c58-delirium-aetiology-categories|This category list is not an exhaustive differential diagnosis.":["u192"],"c58-iatrogenic-allergic-reactions|Allergic reactions are one of the chapter’s examples of iatrogenic problems to consider in the aetiological assessment.":["u193"],"c58-multiple-factors|Removal or treatment of one risk factor alone often fails to resolve delirium.":["u65"],"c58-dementia-risk|Patients with dementia have a two- to fivefold increased risk for delirium.":["u66"],"c58-dementia-share|Fully two-thirds of cases of delirium occur in patients with dementia.":["u67"],"c58-anti-amyloid-historical|SUPERSEDED — DO NOT USE AS CURRENT TREATMENT STATUS.":["u119"],"c58-anti-amyloid-historical|The historical excerpt supplied for review says there were no proven preventive or disease-modifying AD therapies and names aducanumab as the first FDA-approved amyloid-lowering drug without significant cognitive improvement.":["u120"],"c58-anti-amyloid-historical|This excerpt was not present in the preceding reader publication; its Hazzard chapter/page attribution still needs confirmation.":["u121"],"c58-anti-amyloid-historical|The current correction follows.":["u122"],"c58-lecanemab-approval|Lecanemab (Leqembi) received traditional FDA approval on July 6, 2023 after a confirmatory trial verified clinical benefit.":["u123"],"c58-lecanemab-approval|Treatment is initiated in mild cognitive impairment or mild dementia due to Alzheimer disease, with amyloid pathology confirmed; the benefit is slower decline, not restoration of normal cognition.":["u124"],"c58-donanemab-approval|Donanemab (Kisunla) received FDA approval on July 2, 2024.":["u125"],"c58-donanemab-approval|Treatment is initiated in mild cognitive impairment or mild dementia due to Alzheimer disease; the studied population had confirmed amyloid pathology, and treatment slowed clinical decline.":["u126"],"c58-aducanumab-discontinued|Aducanumab (Aduhelm) is not the current treatment example to learn: Biogen announced discontinuation of its development and commercialization on January 31, 2024, and termination of the ENVISION study.":["u127"],"c58-aducanumab-discontinued|Biogen stated that the decision was not based on safety or efficacy concerns; this is not an FDA withdrawal-for-harm finding.":["u128"],"c58-anti-amyloid-scope|These approvals concern selected patients with symptomatic early Alzheimer disease.":["u129"],"c58-anti-amyloid-scope|They do not establish prevention in asymptomatic people or treatment of delirium.":["u130"],"c58-anti-amyloid-scope|Anti-amyloid antibodies carry an ARIA warning, including brain swelling and bleeding that can be serious or life-threatening.":["u131"],"c58-factor-guide|The table separates baseline vulnerability from precipitating factors.":["u111"],"c58-factor-guide|Look for both: several factors may coexist.":["u112"],"c58-medication-contribution|In 30% or more of delirium cases, use of one or more specific medications contributes to its development.":["u70"],"c58-psychoactive-risk|Use of any psychoactive medication is associated with a fourfold increased risk of delirium.":["u71"],"c58-multiple-psychoactive-risk|Use of two or more psychoactive medications is associated with a fivefold increased risk of delirium.":["u72"],"c58-sedative-risk|Sedative-hypnotic drugs are associated with a 3- to 12-fold increased risk of delirium.":["u73"],"c58-narcotic-risk|Narcotics are associated with a threefold increased risk of delirium.":["u74"],"c58-anticholinergic-risk|Anticholinergic drugs are associated with a 5- to 12-fold increased risk of delirium.":["u75"],"c58-delirium-history-triggers|Historical enquiries should include intercurrent illnesses, recent medication adjustments and possible withdrawal from alcohol or other substances.":["u194"],"c58-delirium-history-triggers|These are additions to the baseline and collateral history, not a complete history checklist.":["u195"],"c58-cardiac-presentation|In older adults, acute myocardial infarction and congestive heart failure may present with delirium or failure to thrive as the cardinal feature, with minimal symptoms of angina or dyspnea.":["u76"],"c58-infection-presentation|Occult infection is a particularly noteworthy cause of delirium because older patients may not present with leukocytosis or a typical febrile response.":["u77"],"c58-special-tests-frequency|Cerebrospinal fluid examination, brain imaging, and EEG are probably indicated in fewer than 5% to 10% of delirium cases.":["u78"],"c58-lumbar-puncture|Lumbar puncture with cerebrospinal fluid examination is indicated for the febrile delirious patient when meningitis or encephalitis is suspected.":["u79"],"c58-brain-imaging|Brain imaging should be reserved for delirium cases with new focal neurologic signs, history or signs of head trauma, or without another identifiable cause.":["u80"],"c58-icu-inhospital-mortality|Delirium in the intensive care unit is associated with a fourfold increased risk of in-hospital mortality.":["u81"],"c58-icu-later-mortality|Delirium in the intensive care unit is associated with a sixfold increased risk of mortality at 6 months.":["u82"],"c58-ed-later-mortality|In the emergency department, delirium is associated with a sevenfold increased risk of mortality at 6 months.":["u83"],"c58-symptom-resolution|As few as 20% of patients attain complete delirium symptom resolution at 6-month follow-up.":["u84"],"c58-cognitive-impact|Cognitive function is impacted for up to a year following delirium.":["u85"],"c58-postacute-persistence|Of those admitted to postacute care with delirium, over 50% are still delirious 1 month later.":["u86"],"c58-delirium-supervision-environment|Environmental and supervision strategies include use of sitters, transferring a disruptive patient to a private room, and moving the patient closer to the nurses’ station for increased supervision.":["u196"],"c58-delirium-supervision-environment|These are options to select for the individual situation, not instructions to apply all three to every patient.":["u197"],"c58-nonpharmacologic-first|Nonpharmacologic strategies are the preferred treatment for delirium in older patients, and medications are reserved for more severe symptoms that affect either medical management or patient safety.":["u87"],"c58-targeted-prevention|Implementation of targeted preventive interventions resulted in a 40% risk reduction for delirium in hospitalized older patients compared with standard care.":["u88"],"c58-help-purpose|The Hospital Elder Life Program (HELP), now AGS CoCare HELP, organises hospital care around tested delirium-prevention strategies and improved overall care for older patients.":["u170"],"c58-multicomponent-evidence|At least 14 controlled studies used prospective sampling and validated delirium assessments to examine nonpharmacologic multicomponent primary prevention.":["u171"],"c58-multicomponent-evidence|They found significant reductions in delirium rates, in-hospital falls, healthcare-associated costs and/or delirium duration; not every study demonstrated every outcome.":["u172"],"c58-home-rehabilitation-trial|Another trial associated home rehabilitation after acute hospitalisation with a lower delirium risk and greater patient satisfaction than rehabilitation in an institutional setting.":["u173"],"c58-systems-assessment|System-wide recommendations begin with routine cognitive and functional assessments for all older patients on admission, starting in the emergency department, and monitoring mental status as a vital sign.":["u174"],"c58-systems-education|Educate physicians and nurses to improve recognition and awareness of delirium’s clinical implications, and strengthen geriatric medical and nursing expertise.":["u175"],"c58-systems-practice-change|Create incentives to change practices that promote delirium: immobilisation, deliriogenic medications, bladder catheters and physical restraints.":["u176"],"c58-systems-quality|Build systems supporting geriatric expertise, medication review, family involvement, case management, clinical pathways and quality monitoring for delirium.":["u177"],"c58-hip-fracture-consult|Proactive geriatric consultation has been demonstrated to reduce the risk of delirium post hip fracture by 40% in a randomized controlled trial.":["u89"],"c58-restraints|Physical restraints should be avoided in delirious patients because they lead to decreased mobility, increased agitation, and greater risk of injury and worsening delirium.":["u90"],"c58-sleep-protocol|The nonpharmacologic sleep protocol includes a glass of warm milk or herbal tea, relaxation music or tapes, and back massage.":["u91"],"c58-sleep-medication-use|The nonpharmacologic sleep protocol reduced the use of sleeping medications from 54% to 31% in a hospital environment.":["u92"],"c58-parkinson-lewy-caution|Avoid routine antipsychotic treatment in Parkinson disease and Lewy body dementia.":["u93"],"c58-parkinson-lewy-caution|Severe distress or immediate danger calls for specialist advice and an individually chosen treatment; the Parkinson chapter discusses selected options for persistent psychosis.":["u94"],"c58-iv-caution|If antipsychotic treatment is used, chapter 58 reserves intravenous administration for monitored settings because of torsades and sudden-death risk.":["u113"],"c58-iv-caution|This route discussion is not an intravenous risperidone regimen.":["u114"],"c58-iv-caution|The paragraph discusses parenteral treatment when rapid onset and short duration are needed, while describing oral or intramuscular use as having a more optimal duration of action.":["u115"],"c58-dose-guide|These are chapter 58’s low-dose regimens for selected older patients with delirium, not universal drug maxima across indications.":["u116"],"c58-dose-guide|Medication is reserved for severe symptoms affecting medical care or safety; reassess response and adverse effects before further treatment.":["u117"],"c58-benzodiazepine-caution|Benzodiazepines are not recommended as first-line agents in the treatment of delirium because of their propensity to cause oversedation and exacerbate acute mental status changes.":["u98"],"c58-withdrawal-seizures|Benzodiazepines remain the treatment of choice for delirium caused by seizures and alcohol- and medication-related withdrawal syndromes.":["u99"],"c58-covid-presentation|SARS-CoV-2 and COVID-19 emerged as a global health crisis in early 2020.":["u178"],"c58-covid-presentation|Although the illness commonly presents with influenza-like respiratory symptoms, early neurologic reports included altered mental status.":["u179"],"c58-covid-presentation|In one study of older emergency-department patients with COVID-19, delirium was the sixth most common presenting symptom and sometimes occurred without typical COVID-19 symptoms.":["u180"],"c58-covid-delirium-rates|The chapter reports delirium during COVID-19 hospitalisation in 25–84%.":["u181"],"c58-covid-delirium-rates|These are rates across the cited hospital studies, not a single rate for every patient or pandemic period.":["u182"],"c58-covid-isolation-ppe|Social isolation and personal protective equipment may contribute to more severe or prolonged delirium through poor communication, reduced social interaction and limited reorientation.":["u183"],"c58-covid-ventilation-sedation|Prolonged mechanical ventilation can add immobilisation and deeper sedation.":["u184"],"c58-covid-ventilation-sedation|Drug shortages may lead to second-line medications, adding further barriers to prevention.":["u185"],"c58-covid-prevention-adaptations|Nonpharmacologic prevention was adapted for COVID-19; the chapter directs readers to AGS CoCare HELP resources for these adaptations.":["u186"],"c58-reference-guide|These prevention results share a number but refer to different interventions and populations.":["u100"],"c58-reference-guide|Keep their source sentences separate.":["u101"]}}
